</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Daily closes of the Government of Canada 2-year and 5-year benchmark yields and the Bank of Canada target overnight rate, 265 observations from October 1, 2025 to October 26, 2026. The shaded band is the gap between the 2-year yield and the policy rate. October 27 yields are not yet published.</p>
<p>A two-year yield reflects the expected path of the overnight rate over two years, plus other factors, so the gap is not a forecast of the decision today. It does show that the number most often quoted and the number used to price money are no longer close.</p>
<h2>Anchoring Keeps the Old Number in the Room</h2>
<p>Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman described anchoring in a 1974 paper in Science. They spun a wheel of fortune rigged to stop at 10 or 65 and asked participants to estimate the share of African countries in the United Nations. Median estimates were 25 for the group that saw 10 and 45 for the group that saw 65.</p>
<p>The number on the wheel had nothing to do with the question, and it still pulled the answers toward itself. People start from a salient value and adjust, and the adjustment tends to stop early.</p>
<p>Nicholas Epley and Thomas Gilovich tested why in a 2006 paper in Psychological Science. Adjustment is effortful, and people stop adjusting once they reach a value that feels plausible. The starting point is rarely wrong by much. It is simply the point from which the estimate never fully leaves.</p>
<p>Applied to rates, the implication is direct. If the overnight rate is the starting value, estimates of what money costs will drift toward 2.25% and stop short of where bond markets have been pricing it. The research describes a tendency across people, not a failing in any one of them.</p>
<h2>A Policy Rate Is a Natural Anchor</h2>
<p>The Bank of Canada overnight rate is about the most salient interest rate in the country. It is announced on a calendar, reported in every newspaper and attached to a single, round figure. For 12 months that figure has been 2.25%.</p>
<p>Anchors also work on professionals. Gregory Northcraft and Margaret Neale showed in a 1987 paper in Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes that real estate agents valuing a house were pulled toward its listing price, even though the agents said the listing price played no part in their estimate.</p>
<p>Mortgage borrowers face the same structure. The lowest variable rate sits at 3.43%, according to Ratehub.ca figures reported by MoneySense, and a quarter-point hike would lift it to about 3.7%. A borrower anchored on 2.25% sees a 0.25 point change. A borrower who has watched the two-year yield sees a move that began months ago.</p>
<p>Experience does not fully remove the effect. A borrower who renewed a mortgage in the past two years may be comparing today with a rate set when the two-year yield was near 2.4%, and the 2.25% overnight rate would look consistent with that memory.</p>
<h2>Status Quo Bias and the Quiet Reference Point</h2>
<p>William Samuelson and Richard Zeckhauser documented status quo bias in a 1988 paper in the Journal of Risk and Uncertainty. When a current option is presented as the default, people choose it more often than they do when the same option is presented as one of several alternatives. Standing still feels like a non-decision, even when it is a decision.</p>
<p>Kahneman and Tversky explained part of the reason in their 1979 Econometrica paper on prospect theory. Outcomes are coded against a reference point, and a loss against that reference point weighs more than an equal gain. If the reference point is a rate locked in at 2.25% or a portfolio value from last spring, the market move since February registers as a loss that has not yet been booked.</p>
<p>Fixed mortgage rates are typically priced off Government of Canada bond yields of matching term. The five-year yield stood at 2.70% on October 31, 2025 and 3.58% on October 26, 2026, an increase of 88 basis points. None of that movement required a decision from the Bank of Canada.</p>
<p>The measured facts are narrow. The overnight rate has not changed in a year, and the two-year and five-year yields have both risen by roughly 90 to 100 basis points since February 27. What households and investors do with those facts is a separate question, and the research cited here does not answer it.</p>
<h2>Decision Day Compresses a Year Into One Number</h2>
<p>Today the gap between the two numbers will be announced as a single outcome. A hike to 2.50% would narrow the gap to 87 basis points at Monday closing yields. A hold at 2.25% would leave it at 112. The yield data from Tuesday is not yet published, so both figures are approximate.</p>
<p>Behavioural research does not say which headline will register. It says that the headline will be read against a reference point, and that for many people the reference point is the policy rate they have heard for a year rather than the yield the bond market has been using.</p>
<p>If the Bank of Canada hikes, the policy rate moves toward the market. If it holds, the market has told a different story for eight months and the anchor remains. Either way, the 265 daily observations show the gap opening well before today.</p>',
'<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Many clients are anchored on the 2.25% policy rate they have heard for a year. Those with variable-rate mortgages or renewals coming up are waiting on a single announcement at 9:45 a.m. ET, and may be reading it as the start of a change rather than as a late step in a repricing that bond markets began in the spring.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I know the Bank of Canada announces its decision this morning, and I want to put the number in context. The overnight rate has been 2.25% for a year. The two-year government bond yield closed Monday at 3.37%, so the market has been using a much higher rate than the headline one since the spring. That does not tell us what the Bank will do today. It does tell us that whatever it does, most of the move in borrowing costs has already happened in the bond market. I would rather review your plan with you once the announcement is out than change anything in front of it. Can we book fifteen minutes this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with variable-rate mortgages, or fixed-rate renewals within 12 months. The lowest variable rate is 3.43% and about 3.7% after a quarter-point hike, according to Ratehub.ca.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with both debt and cash holdings, who pay more on borrowing and earn more on short-term deposits.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Savers and retirees with short-term deposits or guaranteed investment certificates coming due, who may renew at higher rates.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List clients with variable-rate mortgages or renewals in the next 12 months, and call the five most exposed before 9:45 a.m. ET.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare two sentences for each outcome of the decision, a hike to 2.50% and a hold at 2.25%.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check which clients hold short-term deposits or GICs maturing in the next 90 days.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Send the follow-up email to every client who has called or written this week.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Book review calls for later this week and document each conversation in the client file.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Where rates stand on decision day<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for the conversation. A short summary of what we covered. The Bank of Canada overnight rate has been 2.25% for a year, while the two-year government bond yield closed Monday at 3.37%. Bond markets began pricing higher rates in the spring, so much of the change in borrowing costs has already happened regardless of the announcement today.<br><br>
I suggest we review your plan together after the decision rather than before it. Does later this week work for a fifteen-minute call?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
'<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Variable-rate mortgage holders:</strong> Households watching the 9:45 a.m. ET announcement, many of whom have been anchored on 2.25% for a year.</p>
<p><strong>Homeowners with renewals in 2026 and 2027:</strong> People whose fixed rate resets against five-year yields that are 88 basis points higher than a year ago.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding cash and short-term deposits:</strong> Self-directed savers deciding whether to lock in or wait, with no one to test the decision against.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Good morning, this is [Your Name]. With the Bank of Canada deciding today, I have been showing people one comparison: the policy rate is 2.25%, but the two-year government bond yield is 3.37%. Do you have two minutes to see what that gap means for your mortgage or your savings?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Rate announcements anchor attention on a single number. The research on anchoring and status quo bias says that the number people carry is often the oldest one available, and that adjustment away from it tends to stop early. A second opinion is most useful when the headline number and the market number have drifted apart.</p>
<p>The gap is concrete: 112 basis points at Monday closing yields, up from 15 a year ago. A prospect managing alone has no one to compare the two against. You can put them side by side.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. When did you last review your mortgage rate, and when does your term end?</p>
<p>2. What rate are you assuming for your next renewal?</p>
<p>3. Do you hold cash or short-term deposits, and when do they mature?</p>
<p>4. How do you decide when to lock in a rate and when to wait?</p>
<p>5. Who do you talk to before you make a change to your borrowing or investments?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Two rates worth comparing today<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its decision this morning. The overnight rate has been 2.25% for a year, while the two-year government bond yield closed Monday at 3.37%. Bond markets have been pricing a higher rate since the spring.<br><br>
I am sharing the comparison with a few people ahead of the announcement. Would a fifteen-minute call this week be useful?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
