VALUES (
'2026/10/28/budget-is-eight-days-away-the-2027-payroll-changes-are-already-set',
'tax',
'article',
'Budget Day Is Eight Days Away. The 2027 Payroll Changes Are Already Set.',
'The base Canada Pension Plan rate falls to 9.5% on January 1, while Employment Insurance premiums rise. For a $70,000 salary the net effect is a gain of $108 for the employee, and none of it depends on the November 5 federal budget.',
'<ul>
<li><strong>Budget 2026 will be tabled on November 5, eight days from now,</strong><span> and the Parliamentary Budget Officer projects a 2026-27 deficit of $63.3 billion before any new measures.</span></li>
<li><strong>The base CPP rate falls from 9.9% to 9.5% on January 1, 2027,</strong><span> which is 4.95% to 4.75% for each of the employee and employer.</span></li>
<li><strong>Employment Insurance rises to $1.64 per $100 of insurable earnings for 2027,</strong><span> with maximum insurable earnings of $70,800 and a maximum employee premium of $1,161.12.</span></li>
<li><strong>At a $70,000 salary the CPP cut saves an employee $133 and EI costs $24.93,</strong><span> a net gain of $108.07 a year before the 2027 earnings ceiling is published.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Productivity Mega Deduction is proposed, not law,</strong><span> and would allow immediate expensing of property acquired on or after September 15, 2026.</span></li>
</ul>',
'<p>The federal budget will be tabled on November 5, eight days from now, Finance Minister François-Philippe Champagne announced on Tuesday, October 20. The Parliamentary Budget Officer published its outlook the same day, with a 2026-27 deficit of $63.3 billion and a note that the projections exclude new measures yet to be announced.</p>
<p>Three changes to 2027 payroll deductions are already set, and none depends on that budget. The base Canada Pension Plan rate falls from 9.9% to 9.5% on January 1, 2027. The Employment Insurance employee premium rises to $1.64 per $100 of insurable earnings from $1.63, and the insurable earnings ceiling rises to $70,800 from $68,900.</p>
<p>For an employee earning $70,000, the CPP cut saves $133 a year and the EI increase costs $24.93, a net gain of $108.07. The same salary costs the employer about $98 less. Those are HDQ calculations from the published rates, using the 2026 CPP earnings ceiling because the 2027 figure has not been announced.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">MAXIMUM EMPLOYEE BASE CPP CONTRIBUTION</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">$3,519 IN 2026</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#c0392b;">▼ 2027: -$142 AT 2026 CEILING</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">ANNUAL MAXIMUM &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2015 TO 2026</span>
</div>
