  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: endX, y: endY + 33, "text-anchor": "end", "font-size": 8, "font-weight": 700, fill: "#1f4e79", "font-family": FF}, "2027 AT 4.75%: " + fmt(v27)));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: endX, y: endY + 43, "text-anchor": "end", "font-size": 7.5, fill: "#666666", "font-family": FF}, "ILLUSTRATIVE, 2026 EARNINGS CEILING"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: margin.left + 8, y: Y(vals[last]) + 11, "text-anchor": "start", "font-size": 7.5, fill: "#888888", "font-family": FF}, "2026 LEVEL"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: margin.left + 8, y: MT + 12, "text-anchor": "start", "font-size": 8, fill: "#444444", "font-family": FF}, D[0][0] + " TO " + D[last][0] + ": +" + fmt(tot) + " (+" + totPct + "%)"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: margin.left + 8, y: MT + 23, "text-anchor": "start", "font-size": 8, fill: "#444444", "font-family": FF}, "LARGEST ANNUAL RISE: +" + fmt(maxRise / 100) + " (" + riseYears.join(", ") + ")"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: margin.left + PW - 8, y: MT + PH - 8, "text-anchor": "end", "font-size": 8, fill: "#444444", "font-family": FF}, "2027 CUT: " + fmt(cut).replace("$-", "-$") + ", THE FIRST DECLINE IN THE SERIES"));
  yt.forEach(function(v){ svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: margin.left - 6, y: Y(v) + 3, "text-anchor": "end", "font-size": 8.5, fill: "#aaaaaa", "font-family": FF}, fmt(v))); });
  D.forEach(function(r, k){ svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: xp(k), y: MT + PH + 14, "text-anchor": "middle", "font-size": 8, fill: "#999999", "font-family": FF}, String(r[0]))); });
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: xp(last + 1), y: MT + PH + 14, "text-anchor": "middle", "font-size": 8, fill: "#999999", "font-family": FF}, "2027"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: margin.left + PW / 2, y: MT + PH + 32, "text-anchor": "middle", "font-size": 7.5, "font-weight": 700, fill: "#bbbbbb", "font-family": FF}, "BASE CPP ONLY, EXCLUDES ENHANCEMENT"));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x: margin.left, y: MT - 6, "text-anchor": "start", "font-size": 7.5, "font-weight": 700, fill: "#bbbbbb", "font-family": FF}, "MAXIMUM ANNUAL EMPLOYEE CONTRIBUTION ($)"));
  _cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Government of Newfoundland and Labrador, year maximum pensionable earnings table (2015 to 2026); Office of the Chief Actuary, 33rd Actuarial Report on the Canada Pension Plan (2027 rate); HDQ calculations. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Each point is the year maximum pensionable earnings less the $3,500 basic exemption, multiplied by the 4.95% employee base rate; the enhanced CPP contribution is excluded. The 2027 point applies the 4.75% rate to the 2026 earnings ceiling of $74,600 because the 2027 ceiling has not been published.</p>
<h2>The Base CPP Cut: Who Saves What on January 1</h2>
<p>Bill C-30, the Spring Economic Update 2026 Implementation Act, received royal assent on June 19, according to Benefits and Pensions Monitor. The combined base rate falls 0.40 percentage points, split 4.95% to 4.75% between the employee and the employer. It applies to earnings between the $3,500 basic exemption and the maximum pensionable earnings, and it does not touch the additional CPP in place since 2019.</p>
<p>Assia Billig, then chief actuary at the Office of the Superintendent of Financial Institutions, found in the 33rd Actuarial Report that the base plan can support the lower rate. Total contributions across about 16 million contributors are expected to fall by more than $3 billion a year.</p>
<p>The chart shows how unusual the change is. The maximum employee base contribution rose every year from 2015 to 2026, from $2,479.95 to $3,519.45, and the 2027 cut is the first decline in that series. The largest single-year rises, $163.35 in 2022 and again in 2026, are roughly the size of the $142.20 cut at the 2026 ceiling.</p>
<p>Self-employed individuals pay both halves of the CPP, so the 0.40 point cut applies in full to them. That is $266 a year on $70,000 of net self-employment income, and $284.40 at the 2026 earnings ceiling.</p>
<h2>Employment Insurance Takes Back About a Fifth</h2>
<p>The Canada Employment Insurance Commission set the 2027 employee rate on September 14 at $1.64 per $100 of insurable earnings. The employer rate is $2.30 per $100, which is 1.4 times the employee rate. The maximum employee premium is $1,161.12, up $38.05, and the maximum employer premium is $1,625.57, up $53.27.</p>
<p>At $70,000 the EI increase of $24.93 offsets about 19% of the $133 CPP saving. For earners above the $70,800 ceiling the increase is capped at $38.05, while the CPP saving keeps growing until earnings reach the CPP ceiling. At the 2026 CPP ceiling of $74,600, the employee saves $142.20 on CPP and pays $38.05 more in EI.</p>
<p>For an incorporated business owner paying a salary at that level, the combined employee and employer picture is a CPP saving of $284.40 against an EI increase of $91.32, a net reduction of $193.08 in the payroll cost of one salary. The figure moves when the 2027 CPP ceiling is published.</p>
<h2>What the Registered Accounts See, and What They Do Not</h2>
<p>RRSP room is built from earned income, which for an employee is employment income before payroll deductions. The CPP and EI changes therefore do not change RRSP room, and they do not change the TFSA dollar limit. The $108.07 net gain for a $70,000 salary is cash flow, and it becomes registered savings only if it is directed there.</p>
<p>The segments differ. An employee sees the net gain through a paycheque, and a self-employed individual sees the full 0.40 point cut.</p>
<p>An owner-manager who takes salary pays less in payroll cost per dollar of salary, while dividends create neither RRSP room nor CPP pensionable earnings. The salary side of a salary and dividend mix is cheaper on January 1 than it was on December 31.</p>
<p>The deadline that matters here is calendar-driven. The new rates apply from the first 2027 pay period, so payroll software, owner-manager salary decisions and 2027 cash flow projections need the 9.5% base rate and the $1.64 EI rate in place before then.</p>
<h2>What Is Not in the Numbers Until November 5</h2>
<p>The Parliamentary Budget Officer, Annette Ryan, projects deficits above $63 billion through 2030-31 and wrote that the two fiscal anchors "have imposed limited fiscal discipline on government spending and borrowing." Champagne said the budget will outline the next steps in the government plan. No specific change to RRSP, TFSA or other registered account rules has been named.</p>
<p>One business measure is already on the table. Prime Minister Mark Carney announced the Productivity Mega Deduction on September 15 at the Canada Investment Summit in Toronto, with draft legislation. It would allow most depreciable property to be fully deducted in the year it becomes available for use, and property acquired on or after September 15, 2026 may qualify. The government puts the cost at about $36 billion over five years.</p>
<p>Champagne called it "one of the most significant changes" to the business tax system in half a century. Brian Ernewein, senior adviser at KPMG Canada, called it a timing difference, comparable to an interest-free loan from the government. Individuals and partnerships with individual members cannot use it to create or increase a loss, and most buildings are excluded. It is a proposal, not law.</p>
<p>For a CCPC with equipment, software or vehicle purchases planned, the open dates are September 15 for acquisition, the year the asset becomes available for use, and November 5 for any change in the budget.</p>',
'<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Business owners and salaried clients are hearing about a federal budget in eight days and a payroll rate cut in two months. Many are unsure which changes are already law, which are only proposed and whether any of it touches their RRSP or TFSA. The practical questions are the first 2027 paycheque, the owner-manager salary decision and whether to time equipment purchases.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Budget day is November 5, and I do not want you waiting on it for the things that are already settled. The base CPP rate drops from 9.9% to 9.5% on January 1, and Employment Insurance goes up a little. On a $70,000 salary that works out to about $108 more in your pocket over the year. It does not change your RRSP room or your TFSA limit. If you own a corporation, the cost of paying yourself a salary falls slightly, and the Productivity Mega Deduction is a separate proposal that is not law yet. Can we spend twenty minutes before November 5 on your 2027 salary and equipment plan?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Incorporated business owners who pay themselves a salary, and self-employed clients who pay both halves of CPP. At the 2026 earnings ceiling the self-employed saving is $284.40 and the owner-manager net payroll saving is about $193.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Salaried employees, who gain about $108 on a $70,000 salary after the EI increase. RRSP room and the TFSA limit are unchanged.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> CCPC owners planning equipment, software or vehicle purchases, if the Productivity Mega Deduction is enacted as proposed.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List incorporated clients who pay themselves a salary and flag those who set 2027 compensation before December 31.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm payroll providers are loading the 9.5% base CPP rate and the $1.64 EI rate for the first 2027 pay period.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Ask CCPC clients about equipment, software or vehicle purchases acquired on or after September 15, 2026.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Diarize November 5 and prepare a one-page update for the afternoon of budget day.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check the 2027 CPP earnings ceiling when the CRA publishes it and update the salary figures.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What is already set for 2027, ahead of the November 5 budget<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for the conversation. A short summary of what we covered. The base CPP rate falls from 9.9% to 9.5% on January 1, 2027, and Employment Insurance premiums rise to $1.64 per $100 of insurable earnings. On a $70,000 salary the net effect is a gain of about $108 for the employee. RRSP room and the TFSA limit are not affected. The federal budget on November 5 may add other measures, and the Productivity Mega Deduction is a proposal that is not yet law.<br><br>
I suggest a twenty-minute call before November 5 to review your 2027 salary and equipment plans. Does next week work?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized tax advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
'<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Incorporated owner-managers:</strong> Business owners who pay themselves a salary and set 2027 compensation in the next two months, and who may be waiting for the budget.</p>
<p><strong>Self-employed professionals:</strong> Individuals who pay both halves of CPP and are not tracking the January 1 rate change.</p>
<p><strong>CCPCs planning capital purchases:</strong> Owners of firms with equipment, software or vehicle spending on the horizon, where the Productivity Mega Deduction timing matters.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Good morning, this is [Your Name]. The federal budget is November 5, but some 2027 payroll changes are already set: the base CPP rate drops to 9.5% on January 1 and EI goes up. If you run a corporation or work for yourself, do you have two minutes to hear what that does to your 2027 salary plan?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Budget days produce noise, and the changes already in law get lost in it. A business owner who sorts the settled from the proposed before November 5 can make salary and purchase decisions on dates rather than headlines.</p>
<p>The numbers are specific: a net gain of about $108 on a $70,000 salary, a net payroll saving of about $193 on a salary at the 2026 earnings ceiling, and a proposed deduction that would apply to property acquired on or after September 15, 2026.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. Do you pay yourself a salary, dividends or a mix, and when do you set it for 2027?</p>
<p>2. Is your payroll provider ready for the January 1 CPP and EI changes?</p>
<p>3. Do you have equipment, software or vehicle purchases planned in the next twelve months?</p>
<p>4. Are you waiting for the November 5 budget before making any compensation or purchase decision?</p>
<p>5. Who reviews your corporate and personal tax planning together?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Three 2027 payroll changes that do not depend on the budget<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The federal budget lands on November 5, but three payroll changes for 2027 are already set. The base CPP rate falls from 9.9% to 9.5% on January 1, Employment Insurance rises to $1.64 per $100, and the EI earnings ceiling rises to $70,800. For a $70,000 salary the net effect is a gain of about $108 for the employee.<br><br>
I am sharing a one-page summary with a few business owners. Would a fifteen-minute call this week be useful?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized tax advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
