VALUES (
'2026/10/28/the-bank-of-canada-is-expected-to-hike-into-core-inflation-below-2',
'economy',
'article',
'The Bank of Canada Is Expected to Hike Into Core Inflation Below 2%',
'Headline inflation is 3.1% on gasoline up 27.8%, while CPI-trim is 1.8% and the economy lost 68,000 jobs in September. The decision at 9:45 a.m. ET today tests whether the Bank looks through energy prices or leans against them.',
'<ul>
<li><strong>Nineteen of 34 economists expect a quarter-point hike to 2.50% today,</strong><span> which would be the first increase since July 2023.</span></li>
<li><strong>Headline CPI is 3.1% but CPI-trim is 1.8% and CPI-median is 1.9%,</strong><span> a 1.3 point gap that is the widest in the 14 months to September.</span></li>
<li><strong>Gasoline is up 27.8% from a year earlier, while CPI excluding gasoline is 2.2%,</strong><span> so energy accounts for nearly all of the headline gap.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canada lost 68,000 jobs in September and unemployment rose to 6.5%,</strong><span> after 42,000 jobs lost in August.</span></li>
<li><strong>Firms expect 3.7% inflation over the next year,</strong><span> and nearly a quarter cannot say when inflation returns to 2%, the largest share on record.</span></li>
</ul>',
'<p>The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision at 9:45 a.m. ET today, and the final Monetary Policy Report of 2026 comes with it. In a Reuters poll of 34 economists conducted October 21 to 23, 19 expect a quarter-point hike to 2.50%, which would be the first increase since July 2023. Money markets priced roughly a 77% chance of a hike, according to Reuters, down from nearly fully priced a week earlier.</p>
<p>The case rests on energy, not on the underlying trend. September headline inflation was 3.1%, with gasoline up 27.8% from a year earlier. CPI-trim, which strips out the largest price swings, was 1.8% and CPI-median was 1.9%, both below the 2% target midpoint.</p>
<p>The labour market points the other way. Canada lost 68,000 jobs in September, after 42,000 in August, and the unemployment rate rose to 6.5%. A hike into that combination is a decision about inflation expectations rather than about current core inflation.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
