<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Year-over-year change in the total consumer price index, CPI-trim and CPI-median, monthly, August 2025 to September 2026. The dashed line is the 2% midpoint of the Bank of Canada 1% to 3% inflation target range, and the shaded band lies below the 3% upper bound.</p>
<h2>What Headline and Core Are Each Saying</h2>
<p>Headline CPI is 1.3 percentage points above CPI-trim, the widest gap in the 14 months charted. A year ago the relationship was reversed: September 2025 headline inflation was 2.4% against CPI-trim of 3.1%.</p>
<p>Energy explains the change. Energy prices rose 19.0% from a year earlier in September and gasoline rose 27.8%, up from 22.8% in August, according to Statistics Canada figures reported by investingLive. CPI excluding gasoline eased to 2.2% from 2.4%. Food bought in stores rose 1.7%, the lowest since May 2024.</p>
<p>Core measures have fallen steadily. CPI-trim was 3.1% in September 2025 and is 1.8% now, and CPI-median fell from 3.1% to 1.9%. CPI-common, which tracks price changes shared across components, eased to 2.4% from 2.6%. By the measures the Bank built to look through one-off moves, underlying inflation is at or below target.</p>
<p>The two readings point to different policy conclusions. Measured by headline inflation, the Bank faces a rate above the 3% ceiling of its 1% to 3% target range for the first time since May, when it reached 3.2%. Measured by core, inflation is 0.1 to 0.2 points under the 2% midpoint and still falling.</p>
<h2>Why the Bank May Not Look Through It</h2>
<p>Governor Tiff Macklem said in mid-October that the longer the Bank waits, the "harder it is to look through increases in energy prices." National Bank economists Taylor Schleich and Ethan Currie wrote that "this all leans toward the BoC delivering its first increase since July 2023."</p>
<p>The argument is about expectations. In the Bank of Canada Business Outlook Survey, conducted August 6 to September 2, nearly a quarter of firms could not say when inflation would return to 2%, the largest share since the question was introduced. RBC analysis put firms one-year inflation expectations at 3.7% in September and two-year expectations at 3.1%.</p>
<p>Claire Fan, senior economist at RBC, noted that half of surveyed firms plan to pass on cost increases to customers, more than last quarter. Pass-through is the mechanism through which a gasoline shock becomes a core inflation problem, and it is the risk a hike is meant to insure against.</p>
<p>Schleich and Currie said West Texas Intermediate crude was about US$90 a barrel in early September and has risen a further 10% to 20% since. They judged that has likely exhausted the patience of the Bank.</p>
<h2>The Labour Market Is the Counterweight</h2>
<p>Statistics Canada reported that employment fell by 68,000 in September, and the unemployment rate rose to 6.5% from 6.4%. The employment rate fell to 60.6%, and the participation rate dropped to 64.8%, its lowest since December 1997 outside 2020. Workers aged 15 to 24 lost 48,000 jobs.</p>
<p>Average hourly wages rose 2.3% from a year earlier, to $37.64, which is below 3.1% inflation. Households are losing purchasing power on the gasoline side and facing a softer job market at the same time, and a rate hike adds a borrowing cost to both.</p>
<p>Economists are divided on how much that matters. RBC economists had a base case of no hikes until 2027 as of last week, although Fan said the risks tilt toward earlier moves. David Doyle at Macquarie Group sees 125 basis points of tightening by the end of 2027.</p>
<p>The losses were uneven. Quebec shed 49,000 jobs and British Columbia 20,000, while Alberta added 23,000. Ontario had the highest unemployment rate among the large provinces at 7.0%.</p>
<h2>What to Watch at 9:45 and in the Report</h2>
<p>The Monetary Policy Report carries the inflation forecast. In July the Bank projected inflation of 2.5% this year and 2.0% next year, and Reuters expects a sharp upward revision to the 2027 figure. A majority of the economists in the Reuters poll also forecast a second hike before the end of March.</p>
<p>Three items in the statement and press conference will matter: how the Bank describes energy pass-through, whether it refers to the neutral rate range, and whether it acknowledges the labour market as a constraint. The U.S. Federal Reserve also decides today, though the Bank of Canada decision carries more weight for Canadian portfolios.</p>
<p>Borrowing costs have moved ahead of the announcement. The five-year Government of Canada yield was 3.58% on October 26, compared with 2.70% on October 31, 2025, according to the Bank of Canada Valet API. Reuters reports that a hike would bring higher bank lending rates and mortgage costs and slower economic growth.</p>',
'<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are hearing three things at once: gasoline prices up 27.8%, a Bank of Canada decision at 9:45 a.m. ET and job losses in the news. Borrowers worry about a hike. Savers and retirees wonder whether it helps them. Many are reading the decision as a signal about the economy rather than as a single rate move.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada announces its decision this morning, and most economists expect a quarter-point hike. Headline inflation is 3.1% because gasoline is up almost 28%. The Bank''s preferred core measures are below 2%. So the Bank is deciding whether to act on the energy shock before it spreads into other prices, at a time when job growth is weak. For borrowers, a hike means higher variable-rate and prime-linked costs. For savers, it means better short-term deposit rates. I would rather review your plan with you after the announcement than react to the headline. Can we book fifteen minutes this week?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with variable-rate mortgages, home equity lines of credit or prime-linked business credit, whose borrowing costs follow the policy rate.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with fixed-rate renewals in the next 12 months. The five-year Government of Canada yield has already risen from 2.70% to 3.58% over the past year.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Savers and retirees with cash, high-interest savings or guaranteed investment certificates, who may earn more on new deposits.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List clients with variable-rate or prime-linked debt and call the most exposed before 9:45 a.m. ET.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare two short statements for the outcomes: a hike to 2.50% and a hold at 2.25%.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with GICs or term deposits maturing in the next 90 days.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review the Monetary Policy Report inflation forecast and note any change to the 2027 projection.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Send the follow-up email to clients who called or wrote this week and book review calls.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Bank of Canada decision and what it means for you<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for the conversation. A short summary of what we covered. Headline inflation is 3.1%, driven mainly by gasoline, while the core measures the Bank watches are below 2%. Most economists expected a quarter-point hike to 2.50% today. For borrowers, that means higher costs on variable-rate and prime-linked debt. For savers, it means better rates on new short-term deposits.<br><br>
I suggest we review your plan together after the announcement. Does later this week work for a fifteen-minute call?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
'<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Variable-rate borrowers:</strong> Households and business owners with variable mortgages, lines of credit or prime-linked loans who are watching the 9:45 a.m. ET decision.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees and savers holding cash:</strong> People with deposits or GICs maturing soon who are asking whether a hike changes where they should park money.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors reacting to headlines:</strong> Self-directed investors weighing gasoline, jobs and rate news with no one to test it against.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Good morning, this is [Your Name]. With the Bank of Canada deciding today, I have been showing people one comparison: headline inflation is 3.1% but the core measures are below 2%, and the country lost 68,000 jobs in September. Do you have two minutes to see what that means for your borrowing or your savings?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A rate decision that arrives with weak jobs and an energy shock sends mixed signals, and mixed signals produce hasty decisions. A prospect who sees the headline, core and labour numbers side by side can separate what is already happening to prices from what the Bank may do about it.</p>
<p>The data are specific: headline CPI 3.1%, CPI-trim 1.8%, 68,000 jobs lost, and a five-year government bond yield that has risen 88 basis points in a year.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. Do you have debt that moves with the Bank of Canada rate, and when does it renew?</p>
<p>2. Do you hold cash or term deposits, and when do they mature?</p>
<p>3. Have the gasoline and rate headlines changed how you are thinking about spending or investing?</p>
<p>4. What would you want in place before the next rate announcement?</p>
<p>5. Who do you talk to before you change your borrowing or savings plan?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Three numbers behind the rate decision today<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada decides on rates this morning. Three numbers frame it: headline inflation is 3.1%, the core measures are below 2%, and Canada lost 68,000 jobs in September. Most economists expected a quarter-point hike.<br><br>
I am sharing a one-page summary with a few people. Would a fifteen-minute call this week be useful?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
