VALUES (
'2026/10/28/brent-is-pricing-a-binary-bet-on-iran-before-the-us-midterms',
'geo',
'article',
'Brent Is Pricing a Binary Bet on Iran Before the U.S. Midterms',
'Brent closed at US$113.88 on October 27 as talks with Iran stalled and ship attacks near Hormuz hit a monthly record. The premium over WTI has nearly tripled since July, so Canadian producers capture less of the move than the headline suggests.',
'<ul>
<li><strong>Brent closed at US$113.88 on October 27,</strong><span> up 35% since July 28 and about 58% above the roughly US$72 level before the war began.</span></li>
<li><strong>Talks between Washington and Tehran are stalled over sanctions relief, the naval blockade and security assurances,</strong><span> according to Al Jazeera, while officials from Saudi Arabia, Pakistan and Turkiye met Iranian officials in Tehran on October 26.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Washington Institute counts 28 attacks on ships near Hormuz in October through the 23rd,</strong><span> the highest monthly total on record, and 113 since February 28.</span></li>
<li><strong>Brent now trades US$13.98 above WTI,</strong><span> up from US$4.83 on July 28, because Brent is up 35% and WTI only 26%.</span></li>
<li><strong>HDQ base case is no settlement before the November 3 midterms,</strong><span> which keeps gasoline inflation, currently 27.8%, in front of the Bank of Canada.</span></li>
</ul>',
'<p>Brent crude closed at US$113.88 a barrel on October 27, about 58% above the roughly US$72 it traded at before the war with Iran began. It reached US$116.72 on October 23, the highest close in the series, and was trading near US$111 early on Wednesday. The price now carries two things at once: stalled talks in Tehran and a record month of attacks on ships near the Strait of Hormuz.</p>
<p>The Canadian link is direct. Gasoline prices rose 27.8% from a year earlier in September, according to Statistics Canada figures reported by investingLive, and the Bank of Canada announces its rate decision at 9:45 a.m. ET today. Canadian energy equities and the Canadian dollar are the other two places where the oil price shows up in a portfolio.</p>
<p>The gap between Brent and WTI has widened from US$4.83 on July 28 to US$13.98. That gap is the most useful number for a Canadian reader.</p>
<p>Brent has climbed 35% since late July while WTI has climbed 26%, and the shaded week of October 5 to 11 is when attacks on ships were at their peak.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
