'<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Energy-heavy DIY investors:</strong> Self-directed investors who bought Canadian energy stocks during the rally and have no rule for when to take profit.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with fuel costs:</strong> Owners of trucking, farming and construction businesses who face higher diesel costs and want to understand how long they last.</p>
<p><strong>Retirees on fixed incomes:</strong> People who are paying more for gasoline and heating and asking whether their portfolio keeps pace with inflation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Good morning, this is [Your Name]. Oil is near US$114 a barrel because talks with Iran have stalled, and the next decision point is the American election on November 3. I have been showing people one chart that compares Brent and Canadian-style WTI crude. Do you have two minutes to see what it means for your energy exposure?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A price driven by a political decision can reverse in a day. A prospect who sees the size of the war premium, and who knows which part of it reaches Canadian producers, can decide how much energy exposure fits a plan rather than react to the headline.</p>
<p>The data are specific: Brent up 35% since July 28, WTI up 26%, 28 ship attacks in October, and a US$13.98 gap between the two benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. How much of your portfolio is in energy, directly or through funds?</p>
<p>2. What would make you sell some of an energy position that has risen?</p>
<p>3. Do fuel costs affect your household or business budget?</p>
<p>4. Do you have debt that renews while inflation is high?</p>
<p>5. Who do you talk to before you change your investments after a news event?</p>
