'market',
'article',
'TSX Has Not Moved in Two Weeks While Brent Climbed 8%',
'The TSX composite closed at 36,171.5 on October 27, inside a 2% closing range for 11 sessions, while Brent rose from US$105.69 to US$113.88. The Bank of Canada decides at 9:45 a.m. ET today with the index 2.1% below its August high.',
'<ul>
<li><strong>The S&amp;P/TSX composite closed at 36,171.5 on October 27, up 227.3 points or 0.63%,</strong><span> and has closed between 35,584.5 and 36,325.0 in each of the last 11 sessions.</span></li>
<li><strong>Brent rose 7.7% over those sessions, from US$105.69 to US$113.88,</strong><span> while the TSX gained 0.5%, so the index has not followed oil higher.</span></li>
<li><strong>Materials and technology led on Tuesday while energy fell,</strong><span> with materials up 1.3% and Celestica up 10.0% at 12:31 p.m. ET, and energy down 1.1%.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada decides at 9:45 a.m. ET today,</strong><span> with money markets pricing about a 78% chance of a hike on October 23, according to The Canadian Press.</span></li>
<li><strong>The index is 2.1% below its August 25 close of 36,957.6,</strong><span> and 0.6% above its 10-week moving average of 35,967.6.</span></li>
</ul>',
'<p>The S&amp;P/TSX composite closed at 36,171.5 on October 27, up 227.3 points or 0.63%. It has closed between 35,584.5 and 36,325.0 in each of the last 11 sessions, a range of about 2%. Over the same sessions Brent crude rose 7.7%, from US$105.69 to US$113.88, so the index has not followed oil higher.</p>
<p>The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision at 9:45 a.m. ET today, and the U.S. Federal Reserve also decides. The TSX enters both decisions in the middle of its range, 2.1% below its August 25 close of 36,957.6, the highest close of the past six months.</p>
<p>Weekly candles show a 7.0% advance since April 27 that stalled after the August high, and the 10-week moving average of weekly closes has slipped from 36,127.2 in the week of October 5 to 35,967.6. The index closed 0.6% above that average on Tuesday.</p>
